      "math_academy_id": "e-13002",
      "problem": "Solve the equation $\\dfrac{1}{2} = \\dfrac{3}{4} + z.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's swap the left and right-hand sides so that the variable is on the left-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2} & =\\frac{3}{4}+z \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}+z & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ z+\\frac{3}{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo isolate $z$, we need to perform the *opposite* of adding $\\dfrac{3}{4}$ to $z$, which is *subtracting* $\\dfrac{3}{4}$ from $z.$ Remember to perform the same operation on the right-hand side.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}z+\\frac{3}{4} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ z+\\frac{3}{4}-\\frac{3}{4} & =\\frac{1}{2}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ z+0 & =\\frac{2}{4}-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ z & =\\frac{2-3}{4} \\\\ z & =-\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
